  "University of California IPM, Pantry Pests (Pest Notes 7452): the Indian meal moth is the most common moth pest of stored food in homes; adults have reddish-brown or copper outer wings with a pale grey-cream base; larvae feed on grains, cereals, flour, dried fruit, nuts, pet food, birdseed and chocolate, and leave silk webbing in the food; mature larvae often leave the food to pupate and may be found on walls and ceilings; adult moths do not feed on stored products; some pantry pests can chew through thin plastic bags and cardboard, so store food in glass, metal or heavy plastic containers with tight lids; freezing infested or new products (0 F for about 4 days) kills all stages; https://ipm.ucanr.edu/PMG/PESTNOTES/pn7452.html",
  "University of Florida Featured Creatures, Indian meal moth (Plodia interpunctella): the common name comes from an early report of the larvae feeding on Indian meal, a historical name for cornmeal; females lay roughly 60 to 400 eggs; https://entnemdept.ufl.edu/creatures/urban/stored/indian_meal_moth.htm"
